WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:14.380
The most beloved of tiny organisms is probably
the tardigrade.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:21.570
Bumbly, cute, chubby, and six legged...they
look like macro animals, hence their common

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.400
names...moss piglet and water bear.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.869
And, look, we love them too.

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:29.720
We loooove them.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.630
And we will talk about them on this channel
a great deal.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:38.530
But we also have a favorite species that you
likely have never heard of.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:41.820
Larger than a tardigrade, though only a single
cell.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:48.010
And the fact that you’ve probably never heard of Stentor is sad because they’re beautiful,

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.670
magnificent, and powerful organisms.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:55.160
So, Stentor...

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.640
It’s a genus of single-celled eukaryote.

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:06.730
There are nineteen known species, but there are probably more than that.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:12.750
Some of them can build mucus houses that they
can hang out in and hide inside of.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:16.550
Some carry algae inside of them that produce
food for them.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:22.450
And all of them are astoundingly large for a single celled organism, some can be as much

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.299
as four milimeters long, big enough to be
seen without a microscope!

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.280
The biggest tardigrades, which have thousands
of cells, are 1.5 mm long.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:45.540
One thing every Stentor has in common is the holdfast organelle which lets them anchor to

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:46.950
a single location.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:54.630
Now, they can swim, but in an undisturbed
culture only a few Stentors will be swimming freely.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:02:01.110
Stentors are actually more dense than water,
which means that they sink, so that holdfast orgnelle

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:06.530
lets them save energy once they find a location
that has plenty of food and oxygen.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:10.860
But just because they’re anchored, that doesn’t mean they can’t move.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.660
Most stentors are also capable of stretching
a huge amount.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:22.420
When attached and feeding, a stentor body can reach to five to ten times their original length.

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:27.500
And that’s when it starts to resemble a trumpet, which is where it gets one of its common names,

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.540
"The Trumpet Animalcule"

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:37.800
The biggest of all the Stentors, and one of
the largest single-celled organisms that exists,

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.300
is Stentor coeruleus.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.280
They can be the length of a rice grain!

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:50.640
There are insects in your back yard that are smaller than these massive, but common unicellular organisms.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:56.300
They’re in freshwater habitats all around
the world, and we here at Journey to the Microcosmos

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:57.800
have a pond where we find them

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.460
all the time.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.700
Surprisingly, they thrive even when it's freezing cold.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:09.260
We have a Stentor culture set from Stentor
coeruleus collected under 20 cms of ice.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:14.920
Possibly, the lack of predators during the
winter keeps their numbers extra high.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.220
Stentor coeruleus is a part of a group called
the ciliates.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.820
As is true of all ciliates, Stentor
coeruleus have hair-like structures called

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:24.820
cilia.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.140
The beating of the cilia propels these cells in the water when they want to move around

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.260
and it also brings food particles, micro-animals,

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:37.849
and single-celled organisms into the cell
mouth where they are taken inside the cell

00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:38.849
to be digested.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:45.349
Just like this multicellular rotifer, which
is swallowed by the giant Stentor coeruleus

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:47.209
and waiting to be digested.

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:53.650
We recorded this struggle for a long time, and even witnessed the rotifer rupturing the Stentor's

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.690
cell membrane multiple times.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:04:03.330
Each time, the Stentor repaired itself and
the rotifer never managed to escape.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.270
After 25 minutes the rotifer ceased its struggle.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:12.000
Just a reminder, rotifers have a simple
brain and a simple nervous system.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:17.440
They can feel, I am not sure about pain
but they can certainly feel stimuli.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:28.020
The striking blue color makes Stentor
coeruleus one of the most beautiful species of the

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.220
genus Stentor.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.930
However, Stentor coeruleus are not just big
and blue. They also have many abilities you

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:41.680
would not expect to find in a unicellular
organism, including regeneration, light avoidance,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.620
food selection, and reaction to mechanical stimuli!

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.950
Stentor coeruleus is most famous for its regenerative
abilities.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:58.040
If a Stentor coeruleus cell is cut in half,
each half will regenerate into a normal looking

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.440
cell, at the half size of the uncut cell.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.539
After healing and reconstructing the missing
parts of the cell, these half-sized cells

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:11.070
will grow to the normal cell size given time
and resources.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:17.320
Even if the cell is cut into a hundred pieces,
each fragment can eventually become a normal

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.590
looking cell.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:22.979
Though, for successful regeneration to occur,
we need two things.

00:05:22.979 --> 00:05:30.000
The cut piece must contain at least part of
the macronucleus and a piece of the cell membrane.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.060
The macronucleus of Stentor coeruleus is visible
even under low magnification, it has the

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.289
look of a beaded necklace, and it extends
along the whole cell.

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:47.690
This macronucleus is highly polyploid, which
means that even a fraction of the macronucleus

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:53.550
will contain thousands of copies of the entire
genome of Stentor coeruleus.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:06:00.039
Even a tiny fragment less than one-one hundredth the size of Stentor coeruleus can reconstitute itself

00:06:00.039 --> 00:06:04.449
in this way if both the cell membrane and
the macronucleus are present.

00:06:04.449 --> 00:06:10.560
Portions of Stentor lacking either macronucleus
or cell membrane only survive for a short time.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.240
And the whole regeneration process takes only around
48 hours.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.150
And you don’t have to take our word for this.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:23.080
Sometimes, when preparing slides, we accidentally
cut these massive cells and the pieces of

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:26.720
Stentor coeruleus are left alive in the slide.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:32.180
Within a day, they’re almost fully regenerated
and after two days, we can’t even tell which

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:35.400
is the fragment and which are the unharmed
cells.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.960
Stentor coeruleus can be cannibalistic as
well.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.490
However, no one has ever managed to record
the initial swallowing.

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:49.889
This is likely because the light of the microscope
disturbs the cells.

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:53.420
Here we see a Stentor making a go of that,

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.080
but luckily for the little one, it's a bit too big of a bite.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:04.300
As for that blue color, we know why, but we
don’t know like WHY.

00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:09.319
They contain a blue pigment, “stentorin”
but we don’t know what purpose this beautifully

00:07:09.319 --> 00:07:11.250
colored chemical serves.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:12.250
We have guesses.

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:16.539
It’s possible stentorin helps Stentor Coeruleus
sense light somehow.

00:07:16.539 --> 00:07:22.620
But there’s also evidence that, when a predatory
single-celled organism touches a Stentor Coeruleus,

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.500
it ejects the pigment like squid ink.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.030
In some cases, scientists have observed the
predatory organism pulling away.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:34.879
Unfortunately, though we have attempted to
record this process for ourselves, after many

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:38.580
hours of attempts, it still hasn’t happened
while recording.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.039
But, remember, this isn’t the only species
of Stentor.

00:07:46.039 --> 00:07:48.650
Here we have Stentor polymorphus.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:53.350
This species is mostly filled with endosymbiotic
algae.

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:59.800
These algae live inside of the Stentor polymorphus
and produce sugar via photosynthesis and give

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.270
some of the extra sugar to Stentor.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:06.530
In exchange, Stentor provides protection for
the algae.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:12.810
Look how small those algae are, if you are surrounded
by thousands of different species of algae

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:18.190
eaters, hiding in one of the biggest things
around would certainly protect you from getting

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:19.190
eaten.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:23.520
Look how big they are, each dot is a Stentor
polymorphus cell!

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.861
These cells came from an aquatic snail, it
had so many Stentor cells on its shell, it

00:08:28.861 --> 00:08:30.140
was visibly green!

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:37.260
Now, we’ve been talking about these beautiful and peculiar organisms for a long time now.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.060
And there is, of course, still more to share.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:44.220
We’ll be working hard to keep our Stentor
cultures alive and healthy.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.230
And we haven’t even talked about the peculiar
way that cilliates genetic code works.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:54.180
And how Stentor is the only Ciliate that uses
the same system for encoding genes into proteins

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:55.580
that you and I do.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.340
We’ll have to leave that for a future video,

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.340
For now, enjoy these big blue beauties.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.080
And hey, thanks for coming on this journey
with us.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:27.460
If you want to see more our master of microscopes,
James, follow @jam_and_germs on Instagram.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:46.150
And if you want to see more from us, that,
my friends, is what the subscribe button is for!

